    "concept": "Linux File Systems - Hard Links, Inodes & Here-Document Redirection",
    "markdown": "- **Step-by-Step Command Analysis:**\n  - 1. `cat alpha` prints `Mathematics`.\n  - 2. `ln alpha beta` creates a **hard link** named `beta` pointing to the exact same inode and data blocks as `alpha`. The inode's link count increments from 1 to 2.\n  - 3. `rm alpha` unlinks `alpha` by removing its directory entry. The link count decreases from 2 to 1. Because the link count is non-zero, the inode and data blocks containing `Mathematics` remain completely intact on disk.\n  - 4. `cat >> beta << SAME`:\n    - `>>` appends standard input to `beta`.\n    - `<< SAME` is a **here-document (heredoc)** redirection with delimiter word `SAME`.\n    - Text between the redirection and `SAME` (`Information Technology`) is read as input and appended to `beta`. The delimiter line `SAME` is discarded and never written to the file.\n  - 5. `cat beta` displays the updated contents of `beta`:\n    ```text\n    Mathematics\n    Information Technology\n    ```\n\n- **Conclusion:** `beta` retains the original content `Mathematics` and has `Information Technology` appended to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **Option B (Mathematics Information Technology)**.",
