    "concept": "Transport Layer - Stream-Oriented Interface of TCP",
    "markdown": "- **TCP Architecture & Service Model:**\n  - TCP is a **byte-stream oriented protocol** (RFC 793).\n  - Unlike UDP (which is message-oriented and enforces a maximum datagram size bounded by IP packet limits of $65,535$ bytes), TCP does not impose an upper limit on the size of data handed down by the application layer.\n  - The application layer can write any arbitrary amount of data (from 1 byte to gigabytes) into the TCP socket send buffer using stream operations such as `write()` or `send()`.\n  - The TCP layer automatically fragments and buffers the continuous byte stream into discrete TCP segments sized according to the Maximum Segment Size (MSS) and Path MTU.\n\n- **Conclusion:** The application layer can pass data of **any size** to the TCP layer.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **Option A (Any size)**.",
