  "go:413": {
    "concept": "Relational Calculus - Tuple Relational Calculus (TRC) Quantifier Equivalence",
    "markdown": "- **Logical Equivalence in First-Order Logic & Relational Calculus:**\n  - The given universal quantification over relation $r$ is:\n    $$\\forall t \\in r \\, (P(t))$$\n  - In first-order logic, the universal quantifier $\\forall$ and existential quantifier $\\exists$ are duals under negation:\n    $$\\forall x \\, \\phi(x) \\equiv \\neg \\exists x \\, (\\neg \\phi(x))$$\n  - Restricting the domain to tuples belonging to relation $r$:\n    $$\\forall t \\in r \\, (P(t)) \\equiv \\neg \\exists t \\in r \\, (\\neg P(t))$$\n  - Meaning: *\"For every tuple $t$ in $r$, $P(t)$ is true\"* is logically equivalent to *\"There does NOT exist any tuple $t$ in $r$ such that $P(t)$ is false\"*.\n\n- **Evaluation of the Roman Numeral Expressions:**\n  - **I. $\\neg \\exists t \\in r \\, (P(t))$:** Means no tuple in $r$ satisfies $P(t)$ (equivalent to $\\forall t \\in r \\, (\\neg P(t))$), which is incorrect.\n  - **II. $\\exists t \\notin r \\, (P(t))$:** Considers tuples outside $r$, which is completely unrelated and domain-unsafe.\n  - **III. $\\neg \\exists t \\in r \\, (\\neg P(t))$:** Exactly matches the standard logical equivalence $\\forall t \\in r \\, (P(t))$.\n  - **IV. $\\exists t \\notin r \\, (\\neg P(t))$:** Considers tuples outside $r$, which is incorrect.\n\n- **Conclusion:** Only expression III is equivalent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **Option C (III only)**.",
    "images": []
  },
