    "concept": "Database Indexing - Primary, Clustering, and Secondary Indexes",
    "markdown": "- **Classification of Database File Indexes:**\n  - **Primary Index:**\n    - Defined on an **ordered** data file on a **key** field (the primary key).\n    - It is a sparse index with one entry per data block.\n  - **Clustering Index:**\n    - Defined on an **ordered** data file on a **non-key** field (ordering field that contains duplicate values).\n    - Data records with identical values for the clustering field are stored contiguously in adjacent disk blocks.\n    - It is a sparse index with one index entry per distinct value of the clustering field.\n  - **Secondary Index:**\n    - Defined on an **unordered** data file (or ordered on a different field).\n    - Can be built on either candidate key fields (dense) or non-key fields.\n\n- **Conclusion:** A clustering index is defined on fields that are **non-key and ordering**.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **Option A (non-key and ordering)**.",
