    "concept": "File Systems - Unix Inode Multi-Level Indexing Structure",
    "markdown": "- **Unix Inode File Allocation Scheme:**\n  - In the traditional Unix file system (UFS), file metadata is stored in an **inode** containing:\n    - 12 direct block pointers (pointing directly to data blocks).\n    - 1 single indirect pointer (pointing to a block of direct pointers).\n    - 1 double indirect pointer (pointing to a block of single indirect pointers).\n    - 1 triple indirect pointer (pointing to a block of double indirect pointers).\n  - This hierarchical multi-level indexing structure is designed to support both very small files efficiently (via direct blocks) and extremely large files (up to several gigabytes/terabytes via indirect blocks).\n  - Hence, the allocation strategy used for large files in Unix is an **extension of indexed allocation** (multi-level indexed allocation).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **Option D (an extension of indexed allocation)**.",
